  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-106493",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Both limits ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $[0,2).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
